Complexity
- Time: \(O(n)\), because every node is visited.
- Auxiliary space: \(O(h)\) for recursion.
For a complete binary tree, \(h=O(\log n)\), so the recursion stack is \(O(\log n)\).
This is correct, but it doesn't exploit the fact that the tree is complete.

Approach 2 — Iterative BFS: Count Nodes Level by Level
Idea
We can also visit every node using a queue.
1. Insert the root into the queue.
2. Remove a node and increment the count.
3. Add its existing children.
4. Continue until the queue becomes empty.
Unlike the previous approach, this uses an explicit queue instead of recursion.
Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int countNodes(Node root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        Queue<Node> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        int count = 0;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            Node curr = q.poll();
            count++;

            if (curr.left != null) {
                q.offer(curr.left);
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                q.offer(curr.right);
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}


