For complete trees, the queue can hold \(O(n)\) nodes in the worst case.
Both Approach 1 and Approach 2 are \(O(n)\). To improve further, we must take advantage of the complete-tree property.

Approach 3 — Optimal Recursive Approach Using Left and Right Heights
This is the approach in your code and the standard optimized solution.
Key idea
A perfect binary tree has every level completely filled:
                 1
               /   \
              2     3
             / \   / \
            4   5 6   7


If its height measured in edges is \(h\), its node count is:
\[
2^{h+1}-1
\]

For example, when \(h=2\):
\[
2^{2+1}-1=8-1=7
\]

So we can count a perfect subtree in constant time after measuring its height.
How do we know whether the subtree is perfect?
Measure two heights:
- binaryLeft(root): number of edges on the path that repeatedly follows the left child.
- binaryRight(root): number of edges on the path that repeatedly follows the right child.
If these are equal, the subtree is perfect because the input is guaranteed to be a complete binary tree.
Otherwise, count recursively:
1 + count(left subtree) + count(right subtree)



Your code
class Solution {

    public int countNodes(Node root) {
        // code here
        if(root==null){
            return 0;
        }

        int left=binaryLeft(root);
        int right=binaryRight(root);

        if(left==right){
            return (2<<left)-1;
        }

        return 1+countNodes(root.left)+countNodes(root.right);
    }

    public int binaryLeft(Node root){
        int count=0;
        Node curr=root;
        while(curr.left!=null){
            count++;
            curr=curr.left;
        }
        return count;
    }

    public int binaryRight(Node root){
        int count=0;
        Node curr=root;
        while(curr.right!=null){
            count++;
            curr=curr.right;
        }
        return count;
    }
}


Your code implements the correct optimization.
Understanding (2 << left) - 1
This expression is equivalent to:
(1 << (left + 1)) - 1


Mathematically:
\[
(2\ll h)-1=2^{h+1}-1
\]

For safer arithmetic, especially with larger heights, use a long intermediate:
return (int)((1L << (left + 1)) - 1);


The return type is still int, as in the GFG signature.

