Why this approach is faster
For a perfect subtree, the code doesn't visit each node. It calculates the answer using a formula.
For a complete but imperfect tree, one subtree can be counted immediately as perfect, while recursion continues into the part that isn't perfect.
Each height measurement costs \(O(h)\). Along the recursive path, there are \(O(h)\) levels to inspect.
Complexity
- Time: \(O(h^2)=O(\log^2 n)\)
- Auxiliary space: \(O(h)=O(\log n)\)
This is much faster than \(O(n)\) for large complete trees.
Important: Equal leftmost and rightmost heights imply a perfect subtree here because the input is complete. That shortcut isn't valid for an arbitrary binary tree.

Approach 4 — Binary Search on the Last Level
This is an alternative optimized approach. It uses the same complete-tree property but counts the final level using binary search.
Idea
Let h be the height in edges.
All levels before the last level are full, so their number of nodes is:
\[
2^h-1
\]

The last level can contain at most \(2^h\) nodes.
Because a complete tree fills the last level from left to right, the occupied positions form a prefix:
Indices:  0  1  2  3
Nodes:    ✓  ✓  ✓  ✗


For our six-node example, three of the four possible positions on the last level are occupied.
We can binary-search the last occupied position. For each candidate position, follow its binary index from the root to check whether that node exists.

Code
class Solution {

    // Height measured in edges along the leftmost path.
    private int leftHeight(Node root) {

        int height = 0;

        while (root.left != null) {
            height++;
            root = root.left;
        }

        return height;
    }

    // Checks whether a node exists at index idx
    // on the last level, which is h edges below root.
    private boolean exists(Node root, int h, long idx) {

        Node curr = root;

        for (int bit = h - 1; bit >= 0; bit--) {

            if (curr == null) {
                return false;
            }

            if ((idx & (1L << bit)) == 0) {
                curr = curr.left;
            } else {
                curr = curr.right;
            }
        }

        return curr != null;
    }

    public int countNodes(Node root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        int h = leftHeight(root);

        long nodesAboveLastLevel = (1L << h) - 1;
        long low = 0;
        long high = (1L << h) - 1;

        // Find how many positions on the last level are occupied.
        while (low <= high) {

            long mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (exists(root, h, mid)) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return (int)(nodesAboveLastLevel + low);
    }
}



