Complexity
- Time: \(O(\log^2 n)\), since binary search performs \(O(\log n)\) checks and each check takes \(O(\log n)\).
- Auxiliary space: \(O(1)\).
This matches the time complexity of Approach 3 but uses constant auxiliary space.

Comparison of All Approaches
Approach	Technique	Time	Auxiliary space
1	Recursive DFS — count every node	\(O(n)\)	\(O(\log n)\) for a complete tree
2	BFS — count every node	\(O(n)\)	\(O(n)\)
3	Left/right height comparison — your code	\(O(\log^2 n)\)	\(O(\log n)\)
4	Binary search on the last level	\(O(\log^2 n)\)	\(O(1)\)
Which should you use?
For GFG and interviews, I recommend Approach 3: your recursive solution. It's short, intuitive once you understand the perfect-subtree formula, and achieves \(O(\log^2 n)\) time.
Approach 4 is useful if an interviewer asks for the same time complexity with constant auxiliary space.

